a remains a Republican-leaning state in presidential politics, and the open seat created by the term-limited incumbent does not change that underlying partisanship. No public polls of the state or expert ratings are available, so the forecast rests on the state's roughly nine-point Republican lean. Headlines so far center on 2028 primary maneuvering rather than the general election.\n\nEarly Democratic attention offers a possible wrinkle: Pete Buttigieg's Indiana roots and his lead in early national field polling could lift the party at the top of the ticket, but nothing yet quantifies that effect in Indiana. Republican primary speculation, including Mike Pence stepping aside and a crowded GOP field, has no clear bearing on the state's general-election margin. Republicans hold a clear edge; a native-son ticket or a sharply favorable national environment would be needed to put Indiana in play.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1505, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2049, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.2014, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.2053, "margin": -9.0}]}